/-!
Source/core/bridge triage for this item.

- `source-facing`: Theorem 23.7 identifies the normal cone of the sublevel set
  `{z | f z ≤ f x}` at a nonminimizing subdifferentiable point `x` with the closure of the
  convex cone generated by the subdifferential `∂f(x)`.
- `core/canonical`: the owner abstractions already present in the project are
  `_root_.subdifferentialAt` on an explicit dual side `N`, the Chapter 1 normal-cone notation
  `N[𝕜, N](· | ·)`, the generated-cone owner `cone[𝕜]`, and mathlib's `closure`.
- `bridge/view`: the source phrase “does not attain its minimum at `x`” is expressed by the
  canonical extrema predicate `¬ IsMinOn f Set.univ x`; no auxiliary minimizer wrapper is
  introduced.

Domain-style sampling used here:
- `_root_.subdifferentialAt` from `Chap05/Definition_23_0_6`;
- `N[𝕜, N](· | ·)` from `Chap01/Definition_2_7_10`;
- `cone[𝕜]` from `Chap01/Definition_2_6_10`;
- `Function.IsProper` from `Chap01/Definition_4_6`.

Primitive data vs derived API:
- primitive inputs: a proper convex function `f`, a base point `x`, nonemptiness of the
  subdifferential at `x`, and failure of global minimality at `x`;
- derived conclusion: the normal cone of the closed sublevel set `{z | f z ≤ f x}` at `x`
  equals the closure of the convex cone generated by `_root_.subdifferentialAt (Y := N) f x`.

Layer target: `source-facing`, stated directly on the chapter's canonical pairing-valued owners.
-/

-- Proof sketch: let `C = {z | f z ≤ f x}`. The nonminimality hypothesis gives a strict sublevel
-- point, so `C` has the same closure as `{z | f z < f x}`. Theorem 23.1 makes every strict
-- sublevel direction `z - x` a negative directional-derivative direction, so `N[𝕜, N](x | C)` is
-- the polar of the cone of directions where `directionalDerivativeAt f x` is negative. Theorem
-- 23.2 identifies the closure of that direction cone with the polar of the cone generated by
-- `subdifferentialAt f x`, and bipolarity then yields the claimed closure formula.
/-- Theorem 23.7: if `f` is a proper convex function, `x` is subdifferentiable, and `x` does not
attain the minimum of `f`, then the normal cone of the sublevel set `{z | f z ≤ f x}` at `x` is
the closure of the convex cone generated by `∂f(x)`. -/
theorem normalCone_sublevel_eq_closure_cone_subdifferentialAt
    {f : E → WithBotTop 𝕜} {x : E}
    (hf_convex : f.IsConvex 𝕜) (hf_proper : f.IsProper)
    (hsub : (_root_.subdifferentialAt (Y := N) f x).Nonempty)
    (hnotmin : ¬ IsMinOn f Set.univ x) :
    N[𝕜, N](x | {z : E | f z ≤ f x}) =
      closure (cone[𝕜] (_root_.subdifferentialAt (Y := N) f x) : Set N) := sorry

end
